  "live_records": {
    "q-106134": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106134",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14235999/assets/8cd1c9ab91a23fae12dd45078ec44ca55e69da8aa787775d7a9583857a84cb71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{11}{6}$ and $\\frac{25}{6},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola.",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{25}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{25}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{36}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
